**How to implement.** The Library window exports the selected entry, the shown entries and the user's whole library, and credits the licensed materials of a selected sample (001 §B 'Export', 'Licence attribution'; 001 §C '"Export my library" writes the user entries to a catalogue folder'; .spec-md §10.1: 'Export my library…' is in the Library window; the S3.3 brief). (1) `OpticalConstructor.Ui/LibraryWindowView.fs`. (a) `LibraryWindowError` gains `LibraryExportRefusedErr of LibraryExportError`, documented, and its `caption` renders it through `LibraryExportError.caption` (step 119). (b) `LibraryWindowModel` gains `entryExportStatus : ControlCaption option`, `None` when the window opens, with a one-line doc. (c) `member this.exportableSelection : LibraryEntryEnvelope option` is, in Browse mode, the selected entry's latest envelope through `entry.entryId.tryToLibraryEntryId` (step 24) and `context.catalogueImports.proxy.tryLatestEnvelope` (step 116), and `None` otherwise, as step 119's is. (d) `LibraryWindowMsg` gains, each documented, `ExportSelectedLibraryEntry of FileName`, `ExportShownLibraryEntries of CatalogueExportTarget` and `ExportMyLibrary of CatalogueExportTarget`. (e) `updateLibraryWindow`. `ExportSelectedLibraryEntry file` is inert without an `exportableSelection`, and otherwise calls `context.catalogueImports.exportEntry` of its id and `file`. `ExportShownLibraryEntries target` calls `exportEntries` over the ids of `m.filteredEntries` (`LibraryWindowView.fs:518`), each `e.entryId.tryToLibraryEntryId`. `ExportMyLibrary target` calls `exportUserLibrary target` (step 117). `Ok` sets `entryExportStatus` to the outcome's `exportedCaption` (step 119) and clears `lastError`; `Error e` sets `lastError` to `Some (LibraryExportRefusedErr e)` and clears `entryExportStatus`. None of them arms or disarms a gate, or changes the selection or the mode. (f) The view. In the verbs row (`LibraryWindowView.fs:1104`), in Browse mode and after step 101's Register catalogue folder… and step 111's Review imports…, through `MaterialsWindowView.verbButtonOn` and the pickers of steps 118 and 119 opened at `context.catalogueImports.exportFolderOpt`: `libraryExportButton` (`Export…`) while `exportableSelection` is `Some`, dispatching `ExportSelectedLibraryEntry`; `libraryExportShownButton` (`Export shown to catalogue…`) while `filteredEntries` is not empty, dispatching `ExportShownLibraryEntries`; and `libraryExportMyLibraryButton` (`Export my library…`), always, its picker titled `Export my library to a catalogue folder`, dispatching `ExportMyLibrary`. Each catalogue target is `CatalogueExportTarget.ofFolder` of the picked folder. `entryExportStatus`, when `Some`, shows in a `TextBlock` carrying `libraryExportStatusText` after step 101's `libraryImportStatusText`. The view panel (`LibraryWindowView.fs:1240`), after the description, holds `MaterialsWindowView.attributionPanel ConstructorUiId.libraryAttributionPanel` over `context.catalogueImports.proxy.attributionLinesOf entry.attributedEntryIds` (steps 113, 114), and nothing on a read error, so a selected sample credits the licensed materials of its layers. (2) `OpticalConstructor.Controls/UiIds.fs`, the Library window group (`UiIds.fs:498`), under one comment line citing spec 0050 S3.3: `libraryExportButton` (`"LibraryExportButton"`), `libraryExportShownButton` (`"LibraryExportShownButton"`), `libraryExportMyLibraryButton` (`"LibraryExportMyLibraryButton"`), `libraryExportStatusText` (`"LibraryExportStatusText"`) and `libraryAttributionPanel` (`"LibraryAttributionPanel"`). (3) Tests, in `LibraryWindowTests`. The export and attribution facts compose `stubContext` (steps 101, 111) over the in-memory double: with `let stores = CatalogueImportProxy.createInMemory ()`, the context takes `samples = stores.samples`, `materials = stores.materials`, `library = stores.libraryEntries` and `catalogueImports = CatalogueImportContext.create stores.proxy`, the Built-in catalogue is imported through its `importFrom (CatalogueProxy.createBuiltIn ())`, and test entries are built with step 113's fixture and put through `BuiltinCatalogue.putSealedEntries`. Files go into fresh GUID folders under `Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "LibraryWindowTests")`. `the export and attribution UiIds of the Library window are the stable intent-named ids`: the five ids' texts. `ExportSelectedLibraryEntry writes the selected entry's file and pins its version`: with `sampleContentOver` `Lab stack` over `userMaterialContent` `Lab glass` selected (`SelectLibraryEntry`), `ExportSelectedLibraryEntry` of `<folder>/Lab stack.sample-json` writes the file, which the context's `readEntryFile` reads back equal to the held envelope. `entryExportStatus` is `Exported Lab stack version 1 to <file>; that version is now pinned.`, and `stores.samples.removeSample` of `Lab stack` answers `SampleVersionPinnedErr`. `ExportShownLibraryEntries writes the shown entries and the user materials they reference`: with the text filter `Lab stack` committed (`CommitLibraryTextFilter`), `ExportShownLibraryEntries` of a target folder writes a catalogue holding exactly `Lab stack` and `Lab glass`, and the status counts 2 entries. `ExportMyLibrary writes every user entry to a catalogue folder`: over `Lab glass`, `Lab stack` and `licensedMaterialContent` `PTB glass`, `ExportMyLibrary` writes a catalogue whose ids are exactly `heldUserEntries ()`'s three, none under `BuiltInProvenance`. `a refused Library export shows its reason as the window message`: over a library holding only the Built-in catalogue, `ExportMyLibrary` sets `lastError` to `Some (LibraryExportRefusedErr (EmptyLibraryExportErr _))`, `entryExportStatus` is `None`, and the target folder does not exist. `the Library window shows the citation of a CC-BY material of the selected sample`: `libraryWindowView` mounted headless, as the class's mounting facts mount the window's view, with `PTB stack` (a `sampleContentOver` `PTB glass`, `CC-BY-4.0`, the citation `A. Author, Test optical constants (2026), doi:10.0000/test`) selected, shows `libraryAttributionPanel` holding `PTB glass: Licence CC-BY-4.0 — cite: A. Author, Test optical constants (2026), doi:10.0000/test`; with a built-in source selected no `libraryAttributionPanel` is present. `Export my library over the app library writes a catalogue folder that a new library registers with every user entry`: over `AppContext.create` of one `TestAppScope.fileLocations` scope, a user material `Lab glass` saved through the context's `materials.saveMaterial` and a sample `Lab stack` over it saved through `samples.saveSample` reach the database seam. `stubContext` updated with the app context's stores and `catalogueImports` takes `ExportMyLibrary` of a target folder under the test output, and its status counts 2 entries. Over a second, fresh scope, a second `AppContext.create`'s `catalogueImports.registerFolder` of that folder MUST answer CatalogueAwaitsChoices with both rows and no entry/log writes; after the fixture explicitly confirms the complete list through settle, materials.tryGetMaterial and samples.tryGetSample MUST resolve both entries unchanged.

**Acceptance.** After the Library window is wired, its Export…, Export shown to catalogue… and Export my library… verbs MUST export through `exportEntry`, `exportEntries` and `exportUserLibrary`, and its view panel MUST credit the licensed materials of a selected sample; ui-smoke MUST pass; ui-tests MUST pass over the selected classes, including `the export and attribution UiIds of the Library window are the stable intent-named ids`, `ExportSelectedLibraryEntry writes the selected entry's file and pins its version`, `ExportShownLibraryEntries writes the shown entries and the user materials they reference`, `ExportMyLibrary writes every user entry to a catalogue folder`, `a refused Library export shows its reason as the window message`, `the Library window shows the citation of a CC-BY material of the selected sample`, `Export my library over the app library writes a catalogue folder that a new library registers with every user entry`, the count rising by 7; and build, elevation and the eleven s14 invariants MUST pass.
